write("research-plans",
 "Most research plans read like contracts written to survive scrutiny. A plan's real job is shared understanding.",
 [("What a plan is for",
   "Everyone should know **which decision the study informs**, **what success looks like**, and **their own role** — when to contribute, when to observe, when to step back.\n\nCalabro's early-career plan had colour-coded transcripts and full sampling criteria. By slide 3, the PM was messaging someone else.",
   cmp("Written to survive scrutiny", ["Full sampling criteria", "Colour-coded analysis plan", "Every method justified in advance"],
       "Written for alignment", ["The decision it informs", "How the team will use the results", "Who does what, and when"]),
   q("What is the real job of a research plan?",
     ["Shared understanding", "Methodological defensibility", "A record for the research repository", "Stakeholder sign-off before fieldwork"],
     "Which decision, what success looks like, and everyone's role.")),
  ("The one question",
   "When a request arrives — “can you run a survey?” — the move is:\n\n> “Yes, I can do that. But first, **what main questions are you hoping to answer with this research?**”\n\nIt shifts the conversation from method to goal, and it surfaces validation requests (“we've decided to build it and want proof people will like it”) without accusing anyone.\n\nIt also positions you as a collaborator rather than a critic.", None,
   q("Why does this question work better than challenging the method?",
     ["It makes you a collaborator, not a critic", "It takes less of the stakeholder's time", "It avoids committing to a timeline", "It gives you grounds to decline the work"],
     "And it moves the conversation from method to goal.")),
  ("Then cascade",
   "Follow-ups once the goal is on the table:\n\n• Where are we in the build?\n• Which decisions are still open?\n• Do we need to track this over time?\n• Are we comparing to something existing?\n\nThese questions decide the method far better than the original request did.", None,
   q("A stakeholder says they've already decided to build it and want proof people will like it. What has the question surfaced?",
     ["A validation request", "A tracking study", "A comparative study", "A prioritisation problem"],
     "Better to find that out before designing the study."))],
 [q("What went wrong with his early-career plan?",
    ["The PM stopped reading by slide 3", "It missed the sampling criteria", "It was rejected by the research lead", "It took too long to write"],
    "Thoroughness isn't the same as alignment."),
  q("Which three things should a plan make clear?",
    ["Decision, success, roles", "Method, sample, timeline", "Budget, scope, deliverable", "Hypothesis, metric, threshold"],
    "That is the alignment a plan exists to create."),
  q("What does the one question shift the conversation from?",
    ["Method to goal", "Timeline to budget", "Output to outcome", "Qualitative to quantitative"],
    "“What main questions are you hoping to answer?”"),
  q("Which follow-up tells you whether you need a baseline?",
    ["Are we comparing to something existing?", "Where are we in the build?", "Which decisions are still open?", "Do we need to track this over time?"],
    "A comparison needs something to compare against."),
  q("Why do plans get over-written in the first place?",
    ["They're built to survive scrutiny", "Templates demand every section", "Researchers enjoy the detail", "Clients pay by the page"],
    "Defensive thoroughness, aimed at the wrong audience.")],
 summary="A plan creates alignment, not a defence. Start with: what questions are you hoping to answer?")

write("how-many-users",
 "For qualitative work the honest answer is “until new sessions stop changing what we know”. Stakeholders still need a planning number.",
 [("Saturation",
   "Saturation is the point where new data stops changing what you know — **not** everyone saying the same thing, but the core themes holding steady.\n\nSigns you've reached it:\n\n• Repetition across sessions\n• Your framework stops changing\n• “What surprised me in that last session?” → “Not much”, two or three times running\n• Edge cases confirm themes instead of opening new questions", None,
   q("What is saturation?",
     ["New data stops changing what you know", "All participants give the same answer", "You reach the planned sample size", "The error margin falls below 5%"],
     "Core themes hold steady; sessions stop reshaping the framework.")),
  ("Planning ranges",
   "Give a range per method and say up front you may stop early or add more:\n\n• Usability test (per group) — **5**, then iterate\n• Cognitive walkthrough / think-aloud — **5–8**\n• Field / ethnographic — **4–10**\n• Concept test — **5–12**\n• In-depth interviews — **6–15**\n• Diary study — **10–20**\n• Qual card sort — **15–30**\n\nDrawn from NN/g, MeasuringU and Sauro. Present them as risk management, not quotas.",
   cmp("Treated as a quota", ["“We promised 15, so run 15”", "Sample size fixed before the question", "Stopping early looks like failure"],
       "Treated as risk management", ["A range, with a reason", "Stop early or extend, deliberately", "Saturation decides the end"]),
   q("How should a planning range be presented to stakeholders?",
     ["As risk management", "As a contractual commitment", "As a statistical requirement", "As the industry standard for that method"],
     "Say up front you may stop early or add more.")),
  ("Quantitative starting points",
   "For quantitative questions, the number follows **the claim you want to make**.\n\nFrom his startup cheat sheet: discovering problems needs far fewer people than estimating a rate, which needs fewer than detecting a small difference between two designs.\n\nSo decide the claim first — “we found problems”, “about X% succeed”, “A beats B” — then size for it.", None,
   q("What determines a quantitative sample size?",
     ["The claim you want to make", "The size of the user base", "The budget for incentives", "The number of variants being tested"],
     "Finding problems, estimating a rate and detecting a difference need very different numbers."))],
 [q("What is the honest answer to “how many users?” for qual work?",
    ["Until sessions stop changing what we know", "Five, for almost any method", "As many as the budget allows", "At least thirty, for reliability"],
    "Saturation, with a planning range for the calendar."),
  q("Which sign suggests saturation?",
    ["Nothing surprised you, twice running", "Participants agree with each other", "You've run the planned number", "The transcripts stop getting longer"],
    "Agreement isn't the test; a stable framework is."),
  q("What is the planning range for in-depth interviews?",
    ["6–15", "5–8", "10–20", "15–30"],
    "Drawn from NN/g, MeasuringU and Sauro."),
  q("Where do the ranges come from?",
    ["NN/g, MeasuringU and Sauro", "Calabro's own client projects", "A meta-analysis of HCI literature", "ISO standards for usability testing"],
    "Established sources, presented as ranges rather than rules."),
  q("Why five for a usability test?",
    ["It's enough to find problems, then iterate", "It is statistically representative", "It matches most recruiting budgets", "Five is the saturation point for all qual work"],
    "Per group, and then you run another round.")],
 summary="Saturation decides when to stop; ranges are for planning, not quotas.")

write("interview-questions",
 "Juniors used to write leading questions. Prompt-engineering habits have swung them to the opposite problem: flat, context-free questions that produce flat answers.",
 [("Two failure modes",
   "**Leading (the old problem)** — “How much do you love the new dashboard?”, example answers inside the question, two questions in one.\n\n**Over-neutral (the new one)** — no scenario or moment in time, the same emotional temperature throughout, a rigid 15-question script that reads like an AI prompt.\n\nNeutrality is one tool, not the goal.",
   cmp("Leading", ["“How much do you love…?”", "Example answers supplied", "Two questions in one"],
       "Over-neutral", ["No scenario or moment", "Flat temperature throughout", "Reads like an AI prompt"]),
   q("What is the newer failure mode in interview guides?",
     ["Over-neutral, context-free questions", "Questions that are too personal", "Too many follow-up probes", "Questions asked in the wrong order"],
     "Flat questions produce flat answers.")),
  ("How to structure the guide",
   "**Don't over-script.** Five broad questions organised by theme beat ten narrow ones. Follow relevant tangents — that's where discoveries are.\n\n**Funnel.** Broad context first, then narrow to specifics.\n\n**One question at a time.** Split double-barrelled questions; people answer only half.\n\n**No example answers.** They anchor the response and shape what follows.", None,
   q("What happens when you ask a double-barrelled question?",
     ["People answer only half of it", "People take longer to respond", "People ask you to repeat it", "People give a more detailed answer"],
     "Split it in two.")),
  ("Framing on purpose",
   "Add an **“if any” escape hatch**: “What problems, if any, did you run into?” lets people say “none” without feeling they've failed you.\n\nAnd deliberate framing is legitimate when a follow-up needs focus: naming a specific moment or scenario is how experienced researchers direct a session without leading it.", None,
   q("What does “if any” do in a question?",
     ["Lets people answer “none” comfortably", "Makes the question more neutral overall", "Prompts a longer answer", "Signals that problems are expected"],
     "It removes the pressure to produce a problem."))],
 [q("What is the funnel structure?",
    ["Broad context first, then specifics", "Specifics first, then generalise", "Easy questions first, hard ones last", "Demographics first, then behaviour"],
    "It gives the specifics somewhere to sit."),
  q("Why avoid example answers in a question?",
    ["They anchor the response", "They make questions too long", "They reveal the hypothesis", "They slow the session down"],
    "And they shape every follow-up that comes after."),
  q("How many broad questions does Calabro suggest over ten narrow ones?",
    ["Five", "Three", "Eight", "Twelve"],
    "Organised by theme, with room to follow tangents."),
  q("Where do discoveries usually come from?",
    ["Relevant tangents", "The prepared question list", "The final wrap-up question", "Post-session surveys"],
    "Which is why over-scripting costs you."),
  q("Is deliberate framing always wrong?",
    ["No — it focuses a follow-up", "Yes, it always leads the participant", "Only in generative research", "Only when the sample is small"],
    "Neutrality is one tool, not the goal.")],
 summary="Five broad questions, one at a time, no examples, “if any” — and framing on purpose when you need focus.")

write("ethics",
 "Talking about ethics isn't a practice. Calabro borrows psychology's APA Ethics Code — adopted in 1953 when that field moved from academia into practice, much like UX today.",
 [("Five principles, applied",
   "**Respect for rights and dignity** — informed consent, the right to withdraw, privacy and confidentiality.\n**Competence** — only take on work you're qualified to do; get help when you aren't.\n**Integrity** — honest reporting: no overclaiming, no cherry-picking.\n**Professional responsibility** — methods that fit the question; accountability for findings.\n**Concern for welfare** — avoid harm and distress; protect vulnerable participants.\n\nCalabro adds **social responsibility**: consider the wider effects of what the research enables.", None,
   q("Which principle covers “no cherry-picking the quotes that support the roadmap”?",
     ["Integrity", "Competence", "Concern for welfare", "Professional responsibility"],
     "Honest reporting, without overclaiming.")),
  ("Consent in practice",
   "Consent must be **understandable, voluntary and revocable** — not a wall of legal text.\n\nStore and share recordings and data to a security standard, and only for as long as they are needed.\n\nRemove rare details that could identify someone, even when the name has been taken off.",
   cmp("Consent theatre", ["A wall of legal text", "Recordings kept indefinitely", "Names removed, rare details left in"],
       "Real consent", ["Understandable and voluntary", "Kept only as long as needed", "Identifying details removed"]),
   q("A transcript has no name but mentions a very unusual job title at a named company. What should you do?",
     ["Remove the identifying detail", "Keep it — the name is already gone", "Ask the participant to approve the quote", "Store it in a restricted folder"],
     "Rare details can identify someone on their own.")),
  ("The research you shouldn't run",
   "Don't run research whose only purpose is to make a decided product look validated.\n\nThat is both an integrity problem and a professional-responsibility one: the method doesn't fit the question, because there is no real question.", None,
   q("A team has decided to ship and asks for a study to prove users like it. Which principles are at stake?",
     ["Integrity and professional responsibility", "Competence and welfare", "Consent and confidentiality", "Social responsibility alone"],
     "Honest reporting, and methods that fit a real question."))],
 [q("Why the APA code specifically?",
    ["Psychology professionalised the same way UX is now", "It is legally binding in most countries", "It was written for interview-based research", "It is the shortest ethics code available"],
    "Adopted in 1953 as the field moved from academia into practice."),
  q("What does “competence” require?",
    ["Getting help for work you aren't qualified to do", "A formal research qualification", "Annual ethics training", "Peer review of every study"],
    "Know the limits of your own practice."),
  q("How long should participant data be kept?",
    ["Only as long as it is needed", "Seven years, for audit purposes", "Until the project ships", "Indefinitely, in anonymised form"],
    "And stored to a security standard."),
  q("What does Calabro add to the APA principles?",
    ["Social responsibility", "Data minimisation", "Participant compensation", "Independent oversight"],
    "Consider the wider effects of what the research enables."),
  q("What makes consent revocable?",
    ["Participants can withdraw afterwards", "They sign a second form at the end", "The recording auto-deletes after a period", "Consent is re-confirmed each session"],
    "The right to withdraw is part of respect for rights and dignity.")],
 summary="Consent, competence, honesty, care — written down, not just meant.")

write("researchops",
 "Research rarely fails for lack of methods or talent. It fails in the hand-offs.",
 [("Where studies fragment",
   "**1. Recruit** — CRM → spreadsheet → outreach\n**2. Schedule** — people, buffers, incentives\n**3. Collect** — recordings, transcripts\n**4. Synthesise** — the evidence chain stays linked, or doesn't\n**5. Tell** — the story should trace back to the evidence\n\nEvery transition that adds admin work is a place for errors, stale participant data and lost evidence.", None,
   q("Where does ResearchOps usually break?",
     ["At the hand-offs between stages", "In the synthesis stage", "During recruiting", "When reporting to stakeholders"],
     "Each transition adds admin work and loses fidelity.")),
  ("Fewer tools isn't the fix",
   "The instinct is to consolidate tools. The actual problem is **incoherent transitions**.\n\n**Audit hand-offs, not tool counts:** what gets re-typed, re-uploaded or re-explained?\n\nPull participant data from where it lives and keep it current, rather than rebuilding a pool for each study. And outreach that looks legitimate and comes from a real person gets more responses.",
   cmp("Common fix", ["Buy one platform", "Reduce the tool count", "Standardise templates"],
       "Actual fix", ["Audit what gets re-typed", "Keep participant data live", "Keep the evidence chain linked"]),
   q("What should an ops audit look for?",
     ["What gets re-typed, re-uploaded or re-explained", "How many tools the team pays for", "How long each study takes end to end", "Which methods are used most often"],
     "The friction lives in the transitions.")),
  ("Analytics, same lesson",
   "From his Pendo rollout playbook: **auto-tagging everything** produces a mass of data nobody can use.\n\nSet the strategy before tagging — decide which top tasks and funnels you need to see, then instrument those.\n\nSame principle as the research chain: decide what you need to learn, then build the pipeline for it.", None,
   q("What goes wrong when you auto-tag everything in an analytics tool?",
     ["You get data nobody can use", "The tool slows the product down", "Tags conflict with each other", "You exceed the event quota"],
     "Strategy before tags; top tasks first."))],
 [q("What does Calabro say research usually fails for?",
    ["Hand-offs, not methods or talent", "Lack of stakeholder buy-in", "Insufficient sample sizes", "Poor question design"],
    "The transitions are where fidelity is lost."),
  q("How should a study be treated?",
    ["As a connected chain of evidence", "As a single deliverable", "As one phase of the design process", "As a repeatable template"],
    "Momentum is lost at each break in the chain."),
  q("What improves recruiting response rates?",
    ["Outreach from a real person that looks legitimate", "Larger incentives", "Shorter screener surveys", "Contacting people on weekdays"],
    "Legitimacy matters more than volume."),
  q("Why not rebuild a participant pool for each study?",
    ["The data goes stale and work is duplicated", "It breaches data protection rules", "Recruiting platforms charge per pool", "Participants resent repeat contact"],
    "Pull from where the data lives and keep it current."),
  q("What should the final story do?",
    ["Trace back to the evidence", "Summarise every session", "Lead with the method", "Include all raw quotes"],
    "The chain holds from recruiting through to telling.")],
 summary="Audit the hand-offs, not the tool count. Keep the evidence chain linked.")

write("honest-artifacts",
 "Journey maps built in a workshop without research let teams invent user emotions and leave feeling user-centred.",
 [("The feeling of learning vs. learning",
   "**UX theatre:** a journey map of guessed emotions, sticky-note personas, “we did the user-centred thing”, internal familiarity treated as evidence.\n\n**Honest version:** a service blueprint of real operations, assumptions labelled as assumptions, a workshop that aligns on the problem, and research that fills the gaps the map exposes.\n\nJourney maps aren't useless — but their polish makes guesses look like findings.",
   cmp("UX theatre", ["Guessed emotions on a map", "Sticky-note personas", "Familiarity treated as evidence"],
       "Honest artifact", ["Blueprint of real operations", "Assumptions labelled as such", "Research fills the exposed gaps"]),
   q("What makes a workshop journey map risky?",
     ["Its polish makes guesses look like findings", "It takes too long to produce", "It can't be updated later", "It only covers the happy path"],
     "The artifact looks like evidence whether or not any was collected.")),
  ("Why a service blueprint instead",
   "A blueprint documents **how the service actually works**: frontstage, backstage, systems, owners and hand-offs — and where it breaks.\n\nThat is usually what the organisation is missing, and it's checkable: people can confirm or correct each step.\n\n**Document, then interpret.** Map the real process first; mark what is evidence and what the room believes.", None,
   q("What does a service blueprint document that a journey map typically doesn't?",
     ["Backstage systems, owners and hand-offs", "The user's emotional highs and lows", "Competitor comparisons", "Future-state opportunities"],
     "It describes real operations, which can be verified.")),
  ("Workshops are for alignment",
   "Design-thinking workshops are great for alignment — diverging and converging on problem framing and priorities.\n\nJust don't call it user research. **Dot-voting ranks the group's opinions, not users' needs.**", None,
   q("What does dot-voting in a workshop actually measure?",
     ["The group's opinions", "User priorities", "Business value", "Technical feasibility"],
     "Useful for alignment, not a substitute for research."))],
 [q("What is the honest alternative to an imagined journey map?",
    ["A service blueprint", "A persona set", "A competitive audit", "An empathy map"],
    "It documents how the service actually works."),
  q("What should be labelled explicitly on any artifact?",
    ["Assumptions", "Data sources", "The facilitator's name", "The revision date"],
    "Separate observed behaviour from what the room believes."),
  q("What is the right order?",
    ["Document, then interpret", "Interpret, then document", "Workshop, then blueprint", "Research, then align"],
    "Map the real process before adding meaning to it."),
  q("Are design-thinking workshops useless, in this view?",
    ["No — they're good for alignment", "Yes, they produce no value", "Only with a trained facilitator", "Only when users attend them"],
    "The error is calling the output research."),
  q("What should research do after the blueprint exists?",
    ["Fill the gaps the map exposes", "Validate the blueprint with users", "Re-run the workshop with more people", "Convert the blueprint into a journey map"],
    "The blueprint shows you where the unknowns are.")],
 summary="Service blueprints over imagined journey maps; workshops align, they don't research.")

write("c-b2b-research",
 "When NDAs, procurement and security clearance block interviews and testing, there is still evidence available.",
 [("Blocked, but not blind",
   "**Often blocked:** recruiting end users, usability testing (IP and NDA), support logs and tickets, analytics (no data yet).\n\n**Still possible:** task analysis and requirements, shadowing people doing similar tasks, interviewing customer success and domain experts, and reading job postings to map roles and tasks.\n\n“Light the problem from many sources and you get a glimpse of the truth.”",
   cmp("Often blocked", ["Recruiting end users", "Usability testing (NDA, IP)", "Support tickets"],
       "Still possible", ["Task analysis", "Shadowing similar roles", "CS and domain experts"]),
   q("What can job postings tell a researcher with no user access?",
     ["Which roles and tasks exist", "How satisfied employees are", "Which competitors are hiring", "What the buying process looks like"],
     "They map the work even when you can't observe it.")),
  ("The buyer is rarely the user",
   "In enterprise there are **two experiences to design**: the customer organisation that buys, and the employees who use it.\n\nTheir criteria differ. The buyer weighs procurement, security and price; the user weighs whether they can get through the day.\n\nDesigning only for the buyer is how enterprise software earns its reputation.", None,
   q("Why does the buyer–user split matter for research?",
     ["They judge the product by different criteria", "Buyers are harder to recruit", "Users rarely attend demos", "Buyers control the research budget"],
     "Two audiences, two experiences to design.")),
  ("Start with trust",
   "Enterprise stakeholders are protective of data, IP and internal politics.\n\n**Small, low-risk first steps build the trust** that later opens access to real users.\n\nOther routes meanwhile: map workflows into a service blueprint, use search trends and public forums to find frequent problems, and build an internal test lab with the colleagues closest to customers.", None,
   q("How do you eventually get access to real users in a protective organisation?",
     ["Build trust with small, low-risk steps", "Escalate through the account manager", "Add a research clause to the contract", "Offer larger participant incentives"],
     "Access follows trust, not requests."))],
 [q("Who can substitute for end users when access is blocked?",
    ["Customer success and domain experts", "Other designers on the team", "The sales team's prospects", "Synthetic personas"],
    "Along with shadowing people doing similar tasks."),
  q("What should workflows be mapped into?",
    ["A service blueprint", "A journey map", "A persona document", "A requirements spreadsheet"],
    "It documents how the work actually runs."),
  q("What is an internal test lab, in this note?",
    ["Colleagues closest to customers, used as testers", "A dedicated usability lab room", "A staging environment for prototypes", "A panel of recruited external users"],
    "Imperfect, but closer than nobody."),
  q("Why use search trends and public forums?",
    ["They reveal frequently reported problems", "They provide representative samples", "They replace usability testing", "They show competitor roadmaps"],
    "Public evidence when private evidence is blocked."),
  q("What is the metaphor for combining weak sources?",
    ["Lighting the problem from many sides", "Triangulating a position at sea", "Assembling a jigsaw puzzle", "Filtering signal from noise"],
    "No single source is sufficient; together they give a glimpse of the truth.")],
 summary="No user access? Task analysis, shadowing, CS experts and job postings — and never mistake the buyer for the user.")
